      "s6-c5-db-dc",
      "Defined Benefit and Defined Contribution Plans",
      "In a defined benefit plan, the employer promises each eligible employee a set payment, usually monthly, for life, starting at retirement. This is also called a pension plan. The employer usually funds and manages it. The employer makes the investment decisions and must make sure enough money is set aside. The employer bears the risk, and the lifetime payout is defined and guaranteed.\n\nIn a defined contribution plan, the employee decides how much to contribute. Each participant has their own account and picks from several investment options. Benefits depend on how much was put in and how the investments did. The employee bears the risk, and the payout is not defined or guaranteed.\n\nParticipants usually contribute part of their pay pre-tax. Employers often match part of it. Contributions and earnings grow tax-deferred until withdrawn, and then they are taxed as ordinary income. The IRS sets contribution limits each year. Many plans let people 50 and older make extra catch-up contributions. People aged 60 to 63 can make a bigger catch-up, called a super catch-up. Benefits may be paid in a lump sum, in installments, or as a lifetime annuity.\n\nThe most common defined contribution plan is the 401(k). Corporate employees put part of their wages into their own accounts, and the employer may match. Other types:\n• Profit-sharing plans: The employer contributes based on company profits. Contributions are not guaranteed and may be skipped in years without profits.\n• 403(b) plans, also called tax-sheltered annuities: Similar to 401(k)s, for employees of nonprofits such as public schools and 501(c)(3) groups like charities and religious organizations. Many 403(b) plans are exempt from ERISA, but employers often choose to follow it.\n\nPeople in employer plans can still contribute to an IRA. Those contributions may or may not be tax-deductible.",
