      "To help smaller companies offer retirement plans, Congress created the Simplified Employee Pension (SEP) IRA and the Savings Incentive Match Plan for Employees (SIMPLE) IRA. They are technically under ERISA but are exempt from many ERISA rules. Only businesses can set up SEP and SIMPLE IRAs.\n\nSEP IRAs can be used by a business of any size but are usually used by small ones. The yearly contribution percentage must be made for each eligible employee. Employees do not contribute to SEP IRAs. The employer may change the contribution percentage at will, which other plans do not allow. That flexibility helps small businesses whose profits go up and down.\n\nA SIMPLE IRA is for small employers with no more than 100 employees. Workers can contribute up to a yearly IRS limit. Workers 50 or older can make catch-up contributions, and people aged 60 to 63 can make super catch-up contributions. The employer must match in one of two ways:\n• 2% of salary for all eligible employees, or\n• A match of up to 3% of salary for employees who take part\n\nEmployer contributions are mandatory. There is no flexibility. The company must make them in good times and bad.",
